You are absolutely right. The issue is that the optimization switched from Files.write (which automatically adds a new line for every list item) to BufferedWriter.write (which writes everything on one single continuous line unless told otherwise).
This caused your output to look like one giant, messy block of text instead of neat rows.
Here is the Fixed AsciiGenerationService.java. I have restored the exact formatting structure by explicitly adding writer.newLine() exactly where the old code did.
